  "prediction": "Answer_9:\nThe winner got 3/4 of the votes.\nThe total number of students who voted is 80.\nSo the winner got 3/4 * 80 = 60 votes.\nThe loser got 80 - 60 = 20 votes.\nThe answer is 20.\n\nAnswer_10:\nSandra ordered 4 times as many red roses as white carnations.\nShe also ordered 200 pink calla lilies, which were 5 times the number of white carnations.\nLet's say the number of white carnations is x.\nThen the number of red roses is 4x.\nThe number of pink calla lilies is 5x.\nThe total number of flowers is 4x + 5x + x = 10x.\nWe know that 10x = 200 + 4x.\nSo 6x = 200.\nx = 200 / 6 = 33.33.\nSince we can't have a fraction of flowers, we'll round up to the nearest whole number.\nSo Sandra ordered 34 white carnations.\nShe ordered 4 * 34 = 136 red roses.\nThe answer is 136.\n\nAnswer_11:\nJan is 30 years old.\nTwo years ago, Mark was 5 years older than half Jan's age.\nSo two years ago, Mark was 5 + (30 / 2) = 5 + 15 = 20 years old.\nNow Mark is 20 + 2 = 22 years old.\nJan is 30 years old.\nSo Jean is 30 + 2 = 32 years old.\nThe answer is 32.\n\nAnswer_12:\nThe first runner runs 60 seconds.\nThe second runner runs 60 - 3 = 57 seconds.\nThe third runner runs 57 - 3 = 54 seconds.\nThe fourth runner runs 54 - 3 = 51 seconds.\nThe total time for the less well-rounded team is 60 + 57 + 54 + 51 = 222 seconds.\nThe total time for the well-rounded team is 55 + 55 + 55 + 55 = 220 seconds.\nThe faster team wins by 222 - 220 = 2 seconds.\nThe answer is 2.\n\nAnswer_13:\nJerry has a 6-sided die.\nThere are 3 even numbers and 3 odd numbers.\nThe probability of rolling an even number is 3/6 = 1/2.\nThe probability of rolling an odd number is 3/6 = 1/2.\nThe probability of rolling a number greater than 3 is 3/6 = 1/2.\nThe probability of rolling two even numbers in a row is (1/2) * (1/2) = 1/4.\nThe difference between the two probabilities is 1/2 - 1/4 = 1/4.\nTo express this as a percentage, multiply by 100.\n1/4 * 100 = 25%.\nThe answer is 25%.\n\nAnswer_14:\nJenna's mom got 20 apples.\nJenna picked half as many apples as her mom.\nSo Jenna picked 20 / 2 = 10 apples.\nThe answer is 10.\n\nAnswer_15:\nThe rats are kept in 3 cages in equal groups.\nEach rat is given 6 pieces of straw.\nSo each cage has 6 / 3 = 2 rats.\nThere are 10 cages of hamsters.\nEach hamster is given 5 pieces of straw.\nSo there are 10 * 5 = 50 hamsters.\nThe total number of small rodents is 2 * 3 + 50 = 56.\nThe total number of straw given to small rodents is 160.\nEach rat gets 6 pieces of straw.\nEach hamster gets 5 pieces of straw.\nSo the number of rats is 160 / 6 = 26.67.\nSince we can't have a fraction of rats, we'll round down to the nearest whole number.\nSo there are 26 rats.\nThe answer is 26.\n\nAnswer_16:\nMandy owes Benedict $100.\nThey agreed to have monthly interest of 2%.\nSo the interest for the first month is 100 * 0.02 = $2.\nThe interest for the second month is 100 + 2 * 0.02 = $2.04.\nThe interest for the third month is 100 + 2.04 * 0.02 = $2.08.\nThe total interest is 2 + 2.04 + 2.08 = $6.12.\nThe total amount Mandy owes is 100 + 6.12 = $106.12.\nThe answer is 106.12.\n\nAnswer_17:\nThe trader buys a bag of wheat for $20.\nHe transports each bag for $2.\nHe sells each bag for $30.\nThe profit per bag is 30 - 20 - 2 = $8.\nThe total profit is 400.\nSo the number of bags sold is 400 / 8 = 50.\nThe answer is 50.\n\nAnswer_18:\nThree friends spent $20.25 on 3 tickets to the fair.\nThey also spent $4.50 less on food than on the tickets.\nSo they spent 20.25 - 4.50 = $15.75 on food.\nThey also went on 2 different rides which costs $33 for each ride.\nSo they spent 2 * 33 = $66 on rides.\nThe total cost is 20.25 + 15.75 + 66 = $102.\nThey agreed to split all the costs evenly.\nSo each of them paid 102 / 3 = $34.\nThe answer is 34.\n\nAnswer_19:\nJohn collects peaches for 3 hours.\nHe can collect 2 peaches a minute.\nSo he collects 2 * 60 = 120 peaches per hour.\nHe collects peaches for 3 hours.\nSo he collects 120 * 3 = 360 peaches.\nThe answer is 360.\n\nAnswer_20:\nDucks need to eat 3.5 pounds of insects each week.\nThere is a flock of ten ducks.\nSo they need 3.5 * 10 = 35 pounds of insects per week.\nThey need 35 / 7 = 5 pounds of insects per day.\nThe answer is 5.\n\nAnswer_21:\nTom plants 10 trees a year.\nHe chops down 2 trees a year.\nSo he has 10 - 2 = 8 trees per year.\nHe starts with 50 trees.\nSo after 10 years, he has 50 + 8 * 10 = 150 trees.\n30% of the trees die.\nSo 0.3 * 150 = 45 trees die.\nHe has 150 - 45 = 105 trees left.\nThe answer is 105.\n\nAnswer_22:\nThe neighbors placed 18 pink plastic flamingos out on Sue's front yard.\nOn Saturday morning, the neighbors took back one third of the flamingos, painted them white, and put these newly painted white flamingos back out on Sue's front yard.\nSo they took back 18 / 3 = 6 flamingos.\nThey left 18 - 6 = 12 flamingos.\nOn Sunday morning, they added another 18 pink plastic flamingos to the collection.\nSo there are 12 + 18 = 30 pink flamingos.\nThere are 6 white flamingos.\nThere are 30 - 6 = 24 more pink plastic flamingos than white plastic flamingos.\nThe answer is 24.\n\nAnswer_23:\nVioletta needs 5 different colors of crayons.\nOne crayon costs $2.\nSo she needs 5 * 2 = $10.\nShe prepared $20 for this purchase.\nSo she will get 20 - 10 = $10 change.\nThe answer is 10.\n\nAnswer_24:\nA pen costs as much as a pencil and eraser combined.\nA pencil costs $1.20.\nAn eraser costs $0.30.\nSo a pen costs 1.20 + 0.30 = $1.50.\n8 pens will cost 8 * 1.50 = $12.\nThe answer is 12.\n\nAnswer_25:\n200 pounds of carrots are to be distributed to 40 restaurants.\nEach restaurant is to receive 2 pounds of carrots.\nSo the total number of pounds of carrots distributed is 40 * 2 = 80 pounds.\nThe number of pounds of carrots not used is 200 - 80 = 120 pounds.\nThe answer is 120.\n\nAnswer_26:\nThere are 23786 inhabitants in the city of Soda.\n8417 men and 9092 women make up 8417 + 9092 = 17509 inhabitants.\nThe rest of the population is made up of children.\nSo the number of children is 23786 - 17509 = 6277.\nThe answer is 6277.\n\nAnswer_27:\nElaine initially had 20 Pokemon cards.\nAfter a month, she collected three times that number.\nSo she collected 3 * 20 = 60 Pokemon cards.\nIn the second month, she collected 20 fewer cards than those she collected in the first month.\nSo she collected 60 - 20 = 40 Pokemon cards.\nIn the third month, she collected twice the combined number of Pokemon cards she collected in the first and second months.\nSo she collected 2 * (60 + 40) = 2 * 100 = 200 Pokemon cards.\nThe total number of Pokemon cards she has now is 20 + 60 + 40 + 200 = 320.\nThe answer is 320.\n\nAnswer_28:\nMark edits an equal number of sentences each week for two different publishers.\nPublisher B pays Mark twice what Publisher A pays.\nPublisher A pays him 5 cents per sentence.\nSo Publisher B pays him 5 * 2 = 10 cents per sentence.\nMark edits a total number of 1000 sentences each week.\nSo he edits 1000 / 2 = 500 sentences for each publisher.\nThe total amount he makes from Publisher A is 500 * 5 = $25.\nThe total amount he makes from Publisher B is 500 * 10 = $50.\nThe total amount he makes in a week is 25 + 50 = $75.\nThe answer is 75.\n\nAnswer_29:\nBaldur gets 5 pails of water every morning.\nHe gets 6 pails of water every afternoon.\nSo he gets 5 + 6 = 11 pails of water per day.\nEach pail contains 5 liters of water.\nSo he gets 11 * 5 = 55 liters of water per day.\nThe answer is 55.\n\nAnswer_30:\nJohn wins an award at work.\nThe award has a 1 time monetary reward of $5000.\nHe gets a 5% raise in salary.\nHe makes 2000 a week, every week of the year.\nHe got the award on the first day of the year.\nSo he gets 52 * 2000 = $104,000 per year.\nHe gets a 5% raise in salary.\nSo he gets 104,000 * 0.05 = $5200 per year.\nThe total amount he gets is 104,000 + 5200 = $109,200 per year.\nThe answer is 109,200.\n\nAnswer_31:\nJosie grows grapes on her 10-acre farm.\nEach acre produces 5 tons of grapes per year.\nSo her farm produces 10 * 5 = 50 tons of grapes per year.\nEach ton of grapes makes 2 barrels of wine.\nSo her farm produces 50 * 2 = 100 barrels of wine per year.\nThe answer is 100.\n\nAnswer_32:\nCarl ate a sandwich every day this week for lunch and used 2 slices of cheese on each sandwich.\nSo he used 2 * 7 = 14 slices of cheese on sandwiches.\nHe ate cheese and egg omelets for breakfast 3 days in the week using one more slice per omelet than he did per sandwich.\nSo he used 3 * 3 = 9 slices of cheese on omelets.\nHe made a big dish of macaroni and cheese to last him several dinners for the week and used 8 slices of cheese in it.\nSo he used 14 + 9 + 8 = 31 slices of cheese.\nThe answer is 31.\n\nAnswer_33:\nJanet had 22 green pens and 10 yellow pens.\nThen she bought 6 bags of blue pens and 2 bags of red pens.\nThere were 9 pens in each bag of blue and 6 pens in each bag of red.\nSo she bought 6 * 9 = 54 blue pens and 2 * 6 = 12 red pens.\nThe total number of pens she bought is 54 + 12 = 66.\nThe total number of pens she has now is 22 + 10 + 66 = 98.\nThe answer is 98.\n\nAnswer_34:\nBrinley has scores of 89, 71, 92, 100 and 86 on the first five tests.\nShe needs to get an average of 93 on the six tests.\nThe total score she needs is 6 * 93 = 558.\nThe total score she has so far is 89 + 71 + 92 + 100 + 86 = 338.\nThe score she needs on the sixth test is 558 - 338 = 220.\nThe answer is 220.\n\nAnswer_35:\nMicheal rode his bike at least 5 times a week and makes 25 kilometers each time.\nHe did that for four weeks.\nSo he rode 5 * 4 = 20 times.\nHe rode 20 * 25 = 500 kilometers in the first four weeks.\nHe rode his bike only 2 times a week, but for 60 kilometers each time, and he did that for 3 weeks.\nSo he rode 2 * 3 = 6 times.\nHe rode 6 * 60 = 360 kilometers in the last three weeks.\nThe total number of kilometers he rode is 500 + 360 = 860 kilometers.\nThe answer is 860.\n\nAnswer_36:\nJoe's bag of Halloween candy has 25 chocolate bars and 80 candied apples.\nEach chocolate bar weighs twice as much as each candied apple.\nSo each chocolate bar weighs 2 * 40g = 80g.\nThe total weight of the chocolate bars is 25 * 80g = 2000g.\nThe total weight of the candied apples is 80 * 40g = 3200g.\nThe total weight of the candy is 2000g + 3200g = 5200g.\nThe answer is 5200.\n\nAnswer_37:\nRose went to the store on Monday and bought 4 cakes.\nTuesday she went to a different store and bought three times that number of cakes.\nSo she bought 3 * 4 = 12 cakes.\nOn Wednesday she went to another store and bought 5 times the number of cakes she did on Tuesday.\nSo she bought 5 * 12 = 60 cakes.\nThe total number of cakes she bought is 4 + 12 + 60 = 76 cakes.\nThe answer is 76.\n\nAnswer_38:\nTwenty dozen cups cost $1200 less than the total cost of half a dozen plates sold at $6000 each.\nSo the total cost of half a dozen plates is 1200 + 20 * 12 * 1 = 1200 + 240 = $1440.\nThe cost of each cup is 1440 / 20 = $72.\nThe answer is 72.",
